{#ch05-0001}
# SKIRNISMOL

{#ch05-0002}
# {_The Ballad of Skirnir_}

{#ch05-0003}
## Introductory Note

{#ch05-0004}
The {_Skirnismol_} is found complete in the {_Codex Regius_}, and through stanza 27 in the {_Arnamagnæan Codex_}. Snorri quotes the concluding stanza. In {_Regius_} the poem is entitled “For Scirnis” (“Skirnir’s Journey”).

{#ch05-0005}
The {_Skirnismol_} differs sharply from the poems preceding it, in that it has a distinctly ballad quality. As a matter of fact, however, its verse is altogether dialogue, the narrative being supplied in the prose “links,” concerning which cf. introductory note to the {_Grimnismol_}. The dramatic effectiveness and vivid characterization of the poem seem to connect it with the {_Thrymskvitha_}, and the two may possibly have been put into their present form by the same man. Bugge’s guess that the {_Skirnismol_} was the work of the author of the {_Lokasenna_} is also possible, though it has less to support it.

{#ch05-0006}
Critics have generally agreed in dating the poem as we now have it as early as the first half of the tenth century; Finnur Jonsson puts it as early as 900, and claims it, as usual, for Norway. Doubtless it was current in Norway, in one form or another, before the first Icelandic settlements, but his argument that the thistle (stanza 31) is not an Icelandic plant has little weight, for such curse-formulas must have traveled freely from place to place. In view of the evidence pointing to a western origin for many or all of the Eddic poems, Jonsson’s reiterated “Digtet er sikkert norsk og ikke islandsk” is somewhat exasperating. Wherever the {_Skirnismol_} was composed, it has been preserved in exceptionally good condition, and seems to be practically devoid of interpolations or lacunæ.

{#ch05-0007}
Freyr, the son of Njorth, had sat one day in Hlithskjolf, and looked over all the worlds. He looked into Jotunheim, and saw there a fair maiden, as she went from her father’s house to her bower. Forthwith he felt a mighty []{.pb n="108"}love-sickness. Skirnir was the name of Freyr’s servant; Njorth bade him ask speech of Freyr. He said:

{#ch05-0008 ref="skm.1"}
::: stanza
“Go now, Skirnir\! | and seek to gain
Speech from my son;
And answer to win, | for whom the wise one
Is mightily moved.”
:::

{#ch05-0009}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0010 ref="skm.2"}
::: stanza
“Ill words do I now | await from thy son,
If I seek to get speech with him,
And answer to win, | for whom the wise one
Is mightily moved.”
:::

{#ch05-0011}
[]{.pb n="109"}{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0012 ref="skm.3"}
::: stanza
“Speak prithee, Freyr, | foremost of the gods,
For now I fain would know;
Why sittest thou here | in the wide halls,
Days long, my prince, alone?”
:::

{#ch05-0013}
{_Freyr spake_}:

{#ch05-0014 ref="skm.4"}
::: stanza
“How shall I tell thee, | thou hero young,
Of all my grief so great?
Though every day | the elfbeam dawns,
It lights my longing never.”
:::

{#ch05-0015}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0016 ref="skm.5"}
::: stanza
“Thy longings, methinks, | are not so large
That thou mayst not tell them to me;
Since in days of yore | we were young together,
We two might each other trust.”
:::

{#ch05-0017}
{_Freyr spake_}:

{#ch05-0018 ref="skm.6"}
::: stanza
“From Gymir’s house | I beheld go forth
A maiden dear to me;
Her arms glittered, | and from their gleam
Shone all the sea and sky.
:::

{#ch05-0019 ref="skm.7"}
::: stanza
[]{.pb n="110"}“To me more dear | than in days of old
Was ever maiden to man;
But no one of gods | or elves will grant
That we both together should be.”
:::

{#ch05-0020}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0021 ref="skm.8"}
::: stanza
“Then give me the horse | that goes through the dark
And magic flickering flames;
And the sword as well | that fights of itself
Against the giants grim.”
:::

{#ch05-0022}
{_Freyr spake_}:

{#ch05-0023 ref="skm.9"}
::: stanza
“The horse will I give thee | that goes through the dark
And magic flickering flames,
And the sword as well | that will fight of itself
If a worthy hero wields it.”
:::

{#ch05-0024}
[]{.pb n="111"}{_Skirnir spake to the horse_}:

{#ch05-0025 ref="skm.10"}
::: stanza
“Dark is it without, | and I deem it time
To fare through the wild fells,
(To fare through the giants’ fastness;)
We shall both come back, | or us both together
The terrible giant will take.”
:::

{#ch05-0026}
Skirnir rode into Jotunheim to Gymir’s house. There were fierce dogs bound before the gate of the fence which was around Gerth’s hall. He rode to where a herdsman sat on a hill, and said:

{#ch05-0027 ref="skm.11"}
::: stanza
“Tell me, herdsman, | sitting on the hill,
And watching all the ways,
How may I win | a word with the maid
Past the hounds of Gymir here?”
:::

{#ch05-0028}
{_The herdsman spake_}:

{#ch05-0029 ref="skm.12"}
::: stanza
“Art thou doomed to die | or already dead,
Thou horseman that ridest hither?
Barred from speech | shalt thou ever be
With Gymir’s daughter good.”
:::

{#ch05-0030}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0031 ref="skm.13"}
::: stanza
“Boldness is better | than plaints can be
For him whose feet must fare;[]{.pb n="112"}
To a destined day | has mine age been doomed,
And my life’s span thereto laid.”
:::

{#ch05-0032}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0033 ref="skm.14"}
::: stanza
“What noise is that | which now so loud
I hear within our house?
The ground shakes, | and the home of Gymir
Around me trembles too.”
:::

{#ch05-0034}
{_The Serving-Maid spake_}:

{#ch05-0035 ref="skm.15"}
::: stanza
“One stands without | who has leapt from his steed,
And lets his horse loose to graze;”
.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  . | .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .
.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .
:::

{#ch05-0036}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0037 ref="skm.16"}
::: stanza
“Bid the man come in, | and drink good mead
Here within our hall;
Though this I fear, | that there without
My brother’s slayer stands.
:::

{#ch05-0038 ref="skm.17"}
::: stanza
[]{.pb n="113"}“Art thou of the elves | or the offspring of gods,
Or of the wise Wanes?
How camst thou alone | through the leaping flame
Thus to behold our home?”
:::

{#ch05-0039}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0040 ref="skm.18"}
::: stanza
“I am not of the elves, | nor the offspring of gods,
Nor of the wise Wanes;
Though I came alone | through the leaping flame
Thus to behold thy home.
:::

{#ch05-0041 ref="skm.19"}
::: stanza
“Eleven apples, | all of gold,
Here will I give thee, Gerth,
To buy thy troth | that Freyr shall be
Deemed to be dearest to you.”
:::

{#ch05-0042}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0043 ref="skm.20"}
::: stanza
“I will not take | at any man’s wish
These eleven apples ever;
Nor shall Freyr and I | one dwelling find
So long as we two live.”
:::

{#ch05-0044}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0045 ref="skm.21"}
::: stanza
“Then do I bring thee | the ring that was burned[]{.pb n="114"}
Of old with Othin’s son;
From it do eight | of like weight fall
On every ninth night.”
:::

{#ch05-0046}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0047 ref="skm.22"}
::: stanza
“The ring I wish not, | though burned it was
Of old with Othin’s son;
In Gymir’s home | is no lack of gold
In the wealth my father wields.”
:::

{#ch05-0048}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0049 ref="skm.23"}
::: stanza
“Seest thou, maiden, | this keen, bright sword
That I hold here in my hand?
Thy head from thy neck | shall I straightway hew,
If thou wilt not do my will.”
:::

{#ch05-0050}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0051 ref="skm.24"}
::: stanza
“For no man’s sake | will I ever suffer
To be thus moved by might;
But gladly, methinks, | will Gymir seek
To fight if he finds thee here.”
:::

{#ch05-0052}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0053 ref="skm.25"}
::: stanza
“Seest thou, maiden, | this keen, bright sword
That I hold here in my hand?[]{.pb n="115"}
Before its blade | the old giant bends,—
Thy father is doomed to die.
:::

{#ch05-0054 ref="skm.26"}
::: stanza
“I strike thee, maid, | with my magic staff,
To tame thee to work my will;
There shalt thou go | where never again
The sons of men shall see thee.
:::

{#ch05-0055 ref="skm.27"}
::: stanza
“On the eagle’s hill | shalt thou ever sit,
And gaze on the gates of Hel;
More loathsome to thee | than the light-hued snake
To men, shall thy meat become.
:::

{#ch05-0056 ref="skm.28"}
::: stanza
“Fearful to see, | if thou comest forth,
Hrimnir will stand and stare,
(Men will marvel at thee;)[]{.pb n="116"}
More famed shalt thou grow | than the watchman of the gods\!
Peer forth, then, from thy prison.
:::

{#ch05-0057 ref="skm.29"}
::: stanza
“Rage and longing, | fetters and wrath,
Tears and torment are thine;
Where thou sittest down | my doom is on thee
Of heavy heart
And double dole.
:::

{#ch05-0058 ref="skm.30"}
::: stanza
“In the giants’ home | shall vile things harm thee
Each day with evil deeds;
Grief shalt thou get | instead of gladness,
And sorrow to suffer with tears.
:::

{#ch05-0059 ref="skm.31"}
::: stanza
“With three-headed giants | thou shalt dwell ever,
Or never know a husband;
(Let longing grip thee, | let wasting waste thee,—)[]{.pb n="117"}
Be like to the thistle | that in the loft
Was cast and there was crushed.
:::

{#ch05-0060 ref="skm.32"}
::: stanza
“I go to the wood, | and to the wet forest,
To win a magic wand;
.  .  .  .  .  .  .  . | .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .
I won a magic wand.
:::

{#ch05-0061 ref="skm.33"}
::: stanza
“Othin grows angry, | angered is the best of the gods,
Freyr shall be thy foe,
Most evil maid, | who the magic wrath
Of gods hast got for thyself.
:::

{#ch05-0062 ref="skm.34"}
::: stanza
“Give heed, frost-rulers, | hear it, giants,
Sons of Suttung,
And gods, ye too,
How I forbid | and how I ban
The meeting of men with the maid,
(The joy of men with the maid.)
:::

{#ch05-0063 ref="skm.35"}
::: stanza
[]{.pb n="118"}“Hrimgrimnir is he, | the giant who shall have thee
In the depth by the doors of Hel;
To the frost-giants’ halls | each day shalt thou fare,
Crawling and craving in vain,
(Crawling and having no hope.)
:::

{#ch05-0064 ref="skm.36"}
::: stanza
“Base wretches there | by the root of the tree
Will hold for thee horns of filth;
A fairer drink | shalt thou never find,
Maid, to meet thy wish,
(Maid, to meet my wish.)
:::

{#ch05-0065 ref="skm.37"}
::: stanza
“I write thee a charm | and three runes therewith,
Longing and madness and lust;
But what I have writ | I may yet unwrite
If I find a need therefor.”
:::

{#ch05-0066}
[]{.pb n="119"}{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0067 ref="skm.38"}
::: stanza
“Find welcome rather, | and with it take
The frost-cup filled with mead;
Though I did not believe | that I should so love
Ever one of the Wanes.”
:::

{#ch05-0068}
{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0069 ref="skm.39"}
::: stanza
“My tidings all | must I truly learn
Ere homeward hence I ride:
How soon thou wilt | with the mighty son
Of Njorth a meeting make.”
:::

{#ch05-0070}
{_Gerth spake_}:

{#ch05-0071 ref="skm.40"}
::: stanza
“Barri there is, | which we both know well,
A forest fair and still;
And nine nights hence | to the son of Njorth
Will Gerth there grant delight.”
:::

{#ch05-0072}
Then Skirnir rode home. Freyr stood without, and spoke to him, and asked for tidings:

{#ch05-0073 ref="skm.41"}
::: stanza
“Tell me, Skirnir, | ere thou take off the saddle,
Or farest forward a step:
What hast thou done | in the giants’ dwelling
To make glad thee or me?”
:::

{#ch05-0074}
[]{.pb n="120"}{_Skirnir spake_}:

{#ch05-0075 ref="skm.42"}
::: stanza
“Barri there is, | which we both know well,
A forest fair and still;
And nine nights hence | to the son of Njorth
Will Gerth there grant delight.”
:::

{#ch05-0076}
{_Freyr spake_}:

{#ch05-0077 ref="skm.43"}
::: stanza
“Long is one night, | longer are two;
How then shall I bear three?
Often to me | has a month seemed less
Than now half a night of desire.”
:::

{#ch05-0078 on="#ch05-0007"}
::::: commentary
[]{.pb n="107"}[]{.pb n="108"}{_Freyr_}: concerning his father, Njorth, and the race of the Wanes in general, cf. {_Voluspo_}, 21 and note. Snorri thus describes Njorth’s family: “Njorth begat two children in Noatun; the son was named Freyr, and the daughter Freyja; they were fair of aspect and mighty. Freyr is the noblest of the gods; he rules over rain and sunshine, and therewith the fruitfulness of the earth; it is well to call upon him for plenty and welfare, for he rules over wealth for mankind. Freyja is the noblest of the goddesses. When she rides to the fight, she has one-half of the slain, and Othin has half. When she goes on a journey, she drives her two cats, and sits in a cart. Love-songs please her well, and it is good to call on her in love-matters.” {_Hlithskjolf_}: Othin’s watch-tower; cf. {_Grimnismol_}, introductory prose. {_He said_}: both manuscripts have “Then Skathi said:” (Skathi was Njorth’s wife), but Bugge’s emendation, based on Snorri’s version, is doubtless correct.
:::::

{#ch05-0079 on="skm.1"}
::::: commentary
{_My son_}: both manuscripts, and many editors, have “our son,” which, of course, goes with the introduction of Skathi in the prose. As the stanza is clearly addressed to Skirnir, the change of pronouns seems justified. The same confusion occurs in stanza 2, where Skirnir in the manuscripts is made to speak of Freyr as []{.pb n="109"}“your son” (plural). The plural pronoun in the original involves a metrical error, which is corrected by the emendation.
:::::

{#ch05-0080 on="skm.4"}
::::: commentary
{_Elfbeam_}: the sun, so called because its rays were fatal to elves and dwarfs; cf. {_Alvissmol_}, 35.
:::::

{#ch05-0081 on="skm.6"}
::::: commentary
{_Gymir_}: a mountain-giant, husband of Aurbotha, and father of Gerth, fairest among women. This is all Snorri tells of him in his paraphrase of the story.
:::::

{#ch05-0082 on="skm.7"}
::::: commentary
Snorri’s paraphrase of the poem is sufficiently close so that his addition of another sentence to Freyr’s speech makes it probable []{.pb n="110"}that a stanza has dropped out between 7 and 8. This has been tentatively reconstructed, thus: “Hither to me   \|   shalt thou bring the maid, / And home shalt thou lead her here, / If her father wills it   \|   or wills it not, / And good reward shalt thou get.” Finn Magnusen detected the probable omission of a stanza here as early as 1821.
:::::

{#ch05-0083 on="skm.8"}
::::: commentary
{_The sword_}: Freyr’s gift of his sword to Skirnir eventually proves fatal, for at the last battle, when Freyr is attacked by Beli, whom he kills bare-handed, and later when the fire-demon, Surt, slays him in turn, he is weaponless; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 53 and note. {_Against the giants grim_}: the condition of this line makes it seem like an error in copying, and it is possible that it should be identical with the fourth line of the next stanza.[]{.pb n="111"}
:::::

{#ch05-0084 on="skm.10"}
::::: commentary
Some editors reject line 3 as spurious.
:::::

{#ch05-0085 on="skm.12"}
::::: commentary
Line 2 is in neither manuscript, and no gap is indicated. I have followed Grundtvig’s conjectural emendation.
:::::

{#ch05-0086 on="skm.13"}
::::: commentary
This stanza is almost exactly like many in the first part of []{.pb n="112"}the {_Hovamol_}, and may well have been a separate proverb. After this stanza the scene shifts to the interior of the house.
:::::

{#ch05-0087 on="skm.15"}
::::: commentary
No gap indicated in either manuscript. Bugge and Niedner have attempted emendations, while Hildebrand suggests that the last two lines of stanza 14 are spurious, 14, 1–2, and 15 thus forming a single stanza, which seems doubtful.
:::::

{#ch05-0088 on="skm.16"}
::::: commentary
{_Brother’s slayer_}: perhaps the brother is Beli, slain by Freyr; the only other references are in {_Voluspo_}, 53, and in Snorri’s paraphrase of the {_Skirnismol_}, which merely says that Freyr’s gift of his sword to Skirnir “was the reason why he was weaponless when he met Beli, and he killed him bare-handed.” Skirnir himself seems never to have killed anybody.[]{.pb n="113"}
:::::

{#ch05-0089 on="skm.17"}
::::: commentary
{_Wise Wanes_}: cf. {_Voluspo_}, 21 and note.
:::::

{#ch05-0090 on="skm.18"}
::::: commentary
The {_Arnamagnæan Codex_} omits this stanza.
:::::

{#ch05-0091 on="skm.19"}
::::: commentary
{_Apples_}: the apple was the symbol of fruitfulness, and also of eternal youth. According to Snorri, the goddess Ithun had charge of the apples which the gods ate whenever they felt themselves growing old.[]{.pb n="114"}
:::::

{#ch05-0092 on="skm.21"}
::::: commentary
{_Ring_}: the ring Draupnir (“Dropper”) was made by the dwarfs for Othin, who laid it on Baldr’s pyre when the latter’s corpse was burned (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 32 and note, and {_Baldrs Draumar_}). Baldr, however, sent the ring back to Othin from hell. How Freyr obtained it is nowhere stated. Andvari’s ring (Andvaranaut) had a similar power of creating gold; cf. {_Reginsmol_}, prose []{.pb n="115"}after stanza 4 and note. Lines 3 and 4 of this stanza, and the first two of stanza 22, are missing in the {_Arnamagnæan Codex_}.
:::::

{#ch05-0093 on="skm.25"}
::::: commentary
The first two lines are abbreviated in both manuscripts.
:::::

{#ch05-0094 on="skm.26"}
::::: commentary
With this stanza, bribes and threats having failed, Skirnir begins a curse which, by the power of his magic staff, is to fall on Gerth if she refuses Freyr.
:::::

{#ch05-0095 on="skm.27"}
::::: commentary
{_Eagle’s hill_}: the hill at the end of heaven, and consequently overlooking hell, where the giant Hræsvelg sits “in an eagle’s guise,” and makes the winds with his wings; cf. {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 37, also {_Voluspo_}, 50. The second line is faulty in both manuscripts; Hildebrand’s emendation corrects the error, but omits an effective touch; the manuscript line may be rendered “And look and hanker for hell.” The {_Arnamagnæan Codex_} breaks off with the fourth line of this stanza.
:::::

{#ch05-0096 on="skm.28"}
::::: commentary
{_Hrimnir_}: a frost-giant, mentioned elsewhere only in {_Hyndluljoth_}, 33. Line 3 is probably spurious. {_Watchman of the gods_}: Heimdall; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 46.[]{.pb n="116"}
:::::

{#ch05-0097 on="skm.29"}
::::: commentary
Three nouns of doubtful meaning, which I have rendered {_rage_}, {_longing_}, and {_heart_} respectively, make the precise force of this stanza obscure. Niedner and Sijmons mark the entire stanza as interpolated, and Jonsson rejects line 5.
:::::

{#ch05-0098 on="skm.30"}
::::: commentary
In {_Regius_} and in nearly all the editions the first two lines of this stanza are followed by lines 3–5 of stanza 35. I have followed Niedner, Sijmons, and Gering. The two words here translated {_vile things_} are obscure; Gering renders the phrase simply “Kobolde.”
:::::

{#ch05-0099 on="skm.31"}
::::: commentary
The confusion noted as to the preceding stanza, and a metrical error in the third line, have led to various rearrangements and emendations; line 3 certainly looks like an interpolation. {_Three-headed giants_}: concerning giants with numerous heads, cf. {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 33, and {_Hymiskvitha_}, 8.[]{.pb n="117"}
:::::

{#ch05-0100 on="skm.32"}
::::: commentary
No gap indicated in the manuscript; Niedner makes the line here given as 4 the first half of line 3, and fills out the stanza thus: “with which I will tame you, / Maid, to work my will.” The whole stanza seems to be either interpolated or out of place; it would fit better after stanza 25.
:::::

{#ch05-0101 on="skm.33"}
::::: commentary
Jonsson marks this stanza as interpolated. The word translated {_most evil_} is another case of guesswork.
:::::

{#ch05-0102 on="skm.34"}
::::: commentary
Most editors reject line 3 as spurious, and some also reject line 6. Lines 2 and 3 may have been expanded out of a single line running approximately “Ye gods and Suttung’s sons.” {_Suttung_}: concerning this giant cf. {_Hovamol_}, 104 and note.[]{.pb n="118"}
:::::

{#ch05-0103 on="skm.35"}
::::: commentary
Most editors combine lines 1–2 with stanza 36 (either with the first two lines thereof or the whole stanza), as lines 3–5 stand in the manuscript after line 2 of stanza 30. {_Hrimgrimnir_} (“The Frost-Shrouded”): a giant not elsewhere mentioned. Line 5, as a repetition of line 4, is probably a later addition.
:::::

{#ch05-0104 on="skm.36"}
::::: commentary
For the combination of this stanza with the preceding one, cf. note on stanza 35. The scribe clearly did not consider that the stanza began with line 1, as the first word thereof in the manuscript does not begin with a capital letter and has no period before it. The first word of line 3, however, is so marked. Line 5 may well be spurious.
:::::

{#ch05-0105 on="skm.37"}
::::: commentary
Again the scribe seems to have been uncertain as to the stanza divisions. This time the first line is preceded by a period, but begins with a small letter. Many editors have made line 2 []{.pb n="119"}into two half-lines. {_A charm_}: literally, the rune Thurs (þ); the runic letters all had magic attributes; cf. {_Sigrdrifumol_}, 6–7 and notes.
:::::

{#ch05-0106 on="skm.40"}
::::: commentary
{_Barri_}: “The Leafy.”[]{.pb n="120"}
:::::

{#ch05-0107 on="skm.42"}
::::: commentary
Abbreviated to initial letters in the manuscript.
:::::

{#ch05-0108 on="skm.43"}
::::: commentary
The superscription is lacking in {_Regius_}. Snorri quotes this one stanza in his prose paraphrase, {_Gylfaginning_}, chapter 37. The two versions are substantially the same, except that Snorri makes the first line read, “Long is one night,   \|   long is the second.”[]{.pb n="121"}
:::::

